{%- comment -%}
  Product card — one template, rendered once per product.

  **This is a single block, not one per product**, exactly as
  `_collection-card` is. The section holds the collection and renders this
  static block inside its own loop, handing each pass the piece it is for:

      {% content_for 'block', type: '_product-card', id: 'product-card',
                     closest.product: product %}

  So eight pieces show eight cards from one set of settings, and the theme
  editor lists it once with the repeat mark. Anything that would have to differ
  per card therefore has no place here; it comes from the product itself.

  Its name is underscored because it is **private**: a merchant never adds it,
  the section places it. Only its children are in an "Add block" list.

  **The children are placed by CSS, not by their order in the tree**, again as
  the collection card's are. `.card--composed` names one grid row `media`, so
  the photograph and the quick-view disc claim it whatever order they are
  dragged into and every other child flows underneath in the order the merchant
  chose. That is what keeps the disc over the photograph while the two remain
  siblings either of which can be removed on its own.

  **`snippets/product-card.liquid` is untouched and stays the card everywhere
  else.** The collection and search templates still render it, so the theme
  carries one card built two ways for as long as those templates are what they
  are. The class names and every `data-card-*` hook are identical between them
  — that is the contract `theme.js` binds to, and it reads them all from the
  `[data-card]` root rather than by walking the tree, which is what lets the
  markup be rearranged like this at all.

  The card root keeps three things that belong to no one child:

    - `data-card-multi`, the no-JavaScript hover swap, gated on the photograph
      count rather than the slide count for the reason recorded in CLAUDE.md.
    - both button labels, so a metal pick can swap between them without a
      translation string in JavaScript.

  It states them itself rather than taking them from the image or the add
  block, because either can be removed and the attributes would go with it.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign product = closest.product
  assign card_photos = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'image'
  assign add_label = settings.card_add_label | default: 'Add to Bag'
  assign sold_out_label = 'products.sold_out' | t
-%}

<div
  class="card card--composed"
  data-card
  {% if card_photos.size > 1 %}data-card-multi{% endif %}
  data-add-label="{{ add_label | escape }}"
  data-sold-out-label="{{ sold_out_label | escape }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {% content_for 'blocks' %}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.product_card.name",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "product-card-image" },
    { "type": "product-card-title" },
    { "type": "product-card-variants" },
    { "type": "product-card-meta" },
    { "type": "product-card-available" },
    { "type": "product-card-price" },
    { "type": "product-card-tax-note" },
    { "type": "product-card-add" },
    { "type": "product-card-quick-view" },
    { "type": "group" }
  ],
  "settings": []
}
{% endschema %}
